leek16 ISA Specification
========================

1. Overview

Word size:       16 bits
Registers:       8 x 16 bit general purpose
Address Width:   16 bits
Memory:          64 KiB (2^16)
Reset PC:        0x0000

2. Registers

R0-7:      16 bit general purpose
PC:        16 bit program counter
SP:        16 bit stack pointer
CND:       1 bit condition flag

3. Memory Layout

Memory spans the full 16 bit address space.

	0x0000 - 0xFFFF

Memory is byte addressable and 16 bit values are stored big-endian.

Example:

	16 bit instruction layout
	instruction = 0x1234
	address @ 0x1000 = 0x12
	address @ 0x1001 = 0x34

4.  Standard Instruction Format

A standard 16-bit (word) instruction:

15               8        5        2        0
+----------------+--------+--------+--------+
|     OPCODE     |   RD   |   RS1  |  RS2   |
+----------------+--------+--------+--------+
      7 bits          3        3        3


5.  Immediate Instruction Format

An immediate instruction occupies 32-bits, or two words.

First word:

15               8        5        2        0
+----------------+--------+--------+--------+
|     OPCODE     |   RD   |   RS   |  N/A   |
+----------------+--------+--------+--------+
      7 bits          3        3        3

Second word

15                                          0
+-------------------------------------------+
|                 Immediate                 |
+-------------------------------------------+
                 16 bits


After a standard instruction:
PC += 2 (for 2 bytes, 16 bits)

After an immediate instruction:
PC += 4 (for 4 bytes, 32 bits)

6. Instructions

ADD
	Syntax:
		R1 := R2 + R3

	Operation:
		R1 = R2 + R3

	Length:
		16 bits

ADDI
	Syntax:
		R1 := R2 + 5 (constant)
		R1 := 5 + R2

	Operation:
		R1 = R2 + IMM16

	Length:
		32 bits

LOADI
	Syntax:
		R1 := 0x1234 (constant)

	Operation:
		R1 = IMM16

	Length:
		32 bits

LOADR
	Syntax:
		R1 <- [R2]
		[R2] -> R1

	Operation:
		R1 = MEMORY[R2]

	Length:
		16 bits

STORER
	Syntax:
		R1 -> [R2]
		[R2] <- R1

	Operation:
		MEMORY[R2] = R1

	Length:
		16 bits


7. Comparisons/Conditionals

Comparisons set the CND register to either 1 or 0, true or false respectively.

	R1 == R2
	R1 != R2
	R1 > R2
	R1 < R2
	R1 >= R2
	R1 <= R2

For example:

	R1 > R2

is equivalent to:

	CND = (R1 > R2)

8. Bit shifting

You can bit shift using the classic << and >>.

For example:

	R1 := R2 >> 4

This shifts the value in R2 to the right by 4 bits and then stores the result in R1.

9. Control Flow

JMP LABEL

	PC = address(LABEL)

BRT LABEL

	if CND == 1:
		JMP LABEL

BRF LABEL

	if CND == 0:
		JMP LABEL

10. Reset/Boot

On reset:

	PC = 0x0000
	SP = 0xFFFE
	CND = 0

We being execution at address 0x0000.
